  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Confidence continues to increase for a brief period of \npotentially dangerous heat early next week, especially Tuesday.\n\nA high pressure ridge currently spanning across the four corners \nregion will expand across much of the central CONUS through this \nweekend and into early next week. The 500mb high looks to reach near \n600dam over the northern plains into the upper Great Lakes region \nMonday before expanding eastward. \n\nWhile temperatures will climb above average on Monday, southerly\nmoisture advection will just be getting established as the \nweekend's high pressure begins to shift off the Atlantic Coast, \nhelping to keep heat index values not too far off from observed \ntemperatures. However, humidity will be on the rise headed into \nTuesday along with 850mb temperatures climbing above the 99th \npercentile for mid-July around 22-24 degC. This would support \nTuesday afternoon temperatures in the upper 80s to mid-90s and \nheat index values reaching up near the triple digits south of \nLake Ontario from the Niagara Escarpment eastward towards the \nnorthern Finger Lakes region. \n\nTwo considerations for this next warm period across western NY are \n1) increasing pressure gradient associated with a deeper low \ncrossing Hudson Bay well to the north will lead to breezy conditions \nTuesday providing relief from the heat, especially northeast of the \nlakes, and 2) this larger system moving into eastern Canada looks to \nbreakdown/disrupt the upper level ridging pattern enough to limit \nthis heat wave to just one day. While temperatures will still remain \nabove average into late next week, western NY looks to end up on the \ncooler side of the trailing cold front late in the week, however \nuncertainty still remains at this time.",
